TY  - JOUR
TI  - Proposals for natura 2000 protection sites in the North and Baltic Sea of Germany - Implementation of wild birds directive and habitats directive
AU  - Kruger, J.-A.
AB  - On the condition that the two Guidelines of Natura 2000 have to be implemented in the area of the Exclusive Economic Zone of the North and Baltic Sea the Federal Republic of Germany is for the first time under direct obligation to designate protection areas. Altogether for 25 bird species, such as Red Throated Diver and Black Throated Diver (Gavia stellata and G. artica) which are species of Appendix 1, and Black Scoter (Melanitta nigra) as Migrating Bird according to the EU Wild Birds Directive, protection areas are to be designated in both North and Baltic Sea. According to the Habitats Directive in both seas the existence of six habitat types and of two marine mammals as well as seven fish species has to be reported. On the basis of available literature and research results the German Federation for Nature Conservation has developed area proposals. For the North Sea these proposals include three areas according to the Wild Birds Directive and five areas referring to the Habitats Directive. Further, the existing FFH reports from the area of the 12 mile-zone have to be completed. For the Baltic Sea the proposals comprise six regions where still areas have to be designated according to the Wild Birds Directive, and seven areas needing additional reporting of marine FFH sites.
