TI  - The integrated management plan for the Elbe estuary: An inter-state approach
AU  - Netz, B.-U.
AU  - Eichweber, G.
AU  - Heinze, G.-M.
AU  - Hochfeld, B.
AU  - Klocke, E.
AB  - Being the largest of the estuaries on the German North Sea coast, the Elbe estuary's significance for nature conservation is very great. Thus, sites totalling 46,770 ha have been given protected status under the European Union's Birds and Habitats Directives. The three German Länder of Hamburg, Lower Saxony and Schleswig-Holstein, the Federal Administration for Waterways and Navigation and the Hamburg Port Authority then decided in 2007 to prepare a joint management plan in accordance with Article 6 of the Habitats Directive for the entire suite of Natura 2000 sites from Geesthacht to the Elbe mouth at Cuxhaven. The size of the planning area, the diversity of habitat types and species, as well as the multitude of user-specific requirements relating to both the Elbe itself and the associated terrestrial areas needed a comprehensive, three-year process of analysis and communication. As a result, a great number of measures have been adopted that are designed to develop the Elbe estuary towards a more natural state, including measures to extend the tidal influence in the long term. It proved possible to achieve consensus in principle on the set of measures among the parties involved in the planning process.
