TY  - JOUR
TI  - Concepts for sea-use planning in the North Sea
AU  - Eisma, D.
AU  - van Hoorn, H.
AU  - de Jong, A.J.
AB  - The North Sea, and particularly its southern part, is strongly affected by the increase in number and in scale of activities such as shipping, fisheries and mineral extraction. These developments also have a strong impact on the coastal regions. Although most of the problems involved also occur elsewhere in coastal seas, in the North Sea they are concentrated on a relatively small and intensively used sea area. This generates a strong need for planning existing as well as future activities, requiring the development of a conceptual framework for the North Sea. A number of possible concepts is discussed and a concept involving the preservation of the natural worth of the North Sea is stressed. For an effective planning of the use of the North Sea it is essential that in the countries bordering the North Sea a coherent body of agreed policies, conditions and regulations is developed as well as a general spatial and structural plan for the North Sea. To achieve this, a combination of two approaches, using the existing organizations as well as creating an International North Sea Commission, has the possible advantage of being the easiest way to develop a common concept and a common policy. © 1979.
