TY  - JOUR
TI  - THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE RAMSAR CONVENTION IN THE WADDEN SEA
AU  - JEPSEN, PU
AB  - As a coherent management and conservation area the Wadden Sea is, with the exception of few limited areas and shipping lanes, designated as a Ramsar Site. However, concerning the delimitation of the sites within the three Wadden Sea countries, there are significant differences regarding the boundaries to the North Sea and the inclusion of islands and adjacent marshlands and polders. The designation of the Wadden Sea as a Ramsar Site meets the criteria for the identification of wetlands of international importance for waterbirds adopted at the Conference of Contracting Parties in 1990. In order to manage and protect the Wadden Sea as an entity, and to improve the ecological assets and the biodiversity of the area, a 'wise use' strategy was developed by the Trilateral Wadden Sea Cooperation. A draft outline of the document was presented at the Ramsar Conference in Montreux, 1990. The main conclusions and recommendations of the common approach by the Wadden Sea states were adopted at the 6th Governmental Conference on the Protection of the Wadden Sea, 1991.
