TY  - JOUR
TI  - ECOLOGICAL TARGETS IN THE WADDEN SEA - DEVELOPMENT OF ECOLOGICAL TARGETS AS A POLITICAL INSTRUMENT - PREVIOUS ATTEMPTS AND THE PRESENT STATE IN THE WADDEN SEA
AU  - DANKERS, N
AU  - DEVLAS, J
AB  - The development of ecological targets as a political and management tool is discussed. After the Wadden Sea has been recognised as an area worth conserving, aims and goals have been rather different both in time and space. In the Netherlands, a guiding principle was developed in which the choice was made for the conservation or where necessary the restoration of the Wadden Sea as a natural area. The development of sectoral management plans based on the guiding principle was the accepted policy. In Germany, the choice was for the establishment of national parks in which human impact was forbidden as far as possible. Because there was no clear goal, targets were developed. These range from aiming at a specified abundance of a number of selected species, to aiming for a natural development without a specific quantitative goal. Other targets are aiming at good background values, both chemical, physical and disturbance. The latest development is the use of descriptive targets and quantified background values. Each method has advantages and disadvantages and we will have to use a mix of them. It is clear that new indicators for sustainability should be developed. These should be structure and process parameters indicating the intrinsic values of ecosystems.
