TY  - JOUR
TI  - EUROPIPE - GAS-PIPELINE PLANNED THROUGH THE WADDEN SEA - THE IMPACT ON BIRDS AND THE IMPLEMENTATION OF NATIONAL AND INTERNATIONAL LAWS AND CONVENTIONS ON NATURE CONSERVATION
AU  - BACHERT, S
AB  - It is planned to lay the Europipe gas pipeline through the Wadden Sea and even through a national park despite the Trilateral Joint Declaration of Copenhagen of 1982, and the results of the Trilateral Government Conference held in Esbjerg in 1991. Other ways of securing gas and energy supplies without spoiling the Wadden Sea have always existed. The importance of the Wadden Sea, particularly for birds, is acknowledged by a series of international agreements and conventions. On a national basis regulations have also been enacted to provide appropriate and sufficient protection for the region against further economic demands and damage. Due to this interference, the credibility and international standard of nature conservation are being undermined and also a variety of species and habitats are exposed to further threats and disturbances. The following subjects will be discussed in this paper: disturbance of some species of breeding and resting birds, mentioned in Annex I of the EC Bird Directive due to this project; the problems of implementing national law and international conventions on nature conservation; the effect of impact on local people. Finally, some possible solutions are presented, which also take European and international dimensions into consideration.
