AB  - Application of Legal Species Protection in Planning Procedures - Limits and options After the Small Amendment of the Federal Act on Nature Conservation in 2007, supported by several decisions of national courts, species protection is meanwhile playing a central role in planning procedures. This calls upon practical planning to find solutions which are both legally safe but also practicable in order to guarantee the compatibility of planning and species protection. The paper outlines the legal requirements for species protection assessments and provides solutions for different problems of species protection using select examples. Using current planning projects the different types of prohibitions are explained using examples of European bird species and bats, the fish species houting (Coregonus oxyrinchus) and the plant species Elbe Water dropwort (Oenanthe conioides). These investigations show that the existing legislation and supportive interpretations are not able yet to answer all questions of species protection. This makes clear that determinations of nature conservation values hit the walls due to knowledge gaps regarding the relationship of ecology and population biology.
