TY  - JOUR
TI  - Fifty years of experience with the implementation of artificial sand nourishment techniques on the East Frisian barrier islands, Germany
AU  - Kunz, H.
AU  - Stephan, H.-J.
AB  - The East Frisian islands are located in the western part of the German North Sea coast. As barrier islands they shelter the tidal flats of the Wadden Sea and the mainland coast with it's low lying hinterland. The islands are developed for tourism and healthcare. Dune, beach and shoreface nourishments are implemented to protect the islands against storm surges and to compensate for erosion. The artificial sand fills are often implemented within systems of groynes and seawalls. These protection means are part of the German coastal defence system against flooding by storm surges and against land losses by erosion and they are combined with the stabilization works for the shipways in the Ems- and Jade-estuary. Additionally beach restoration is carried out to provide high and wide beaches for recreational purposes. The paper compiles the artificial sand nourishment projects which had been carried out on the East Frisian islands since the beginning fifty years ago. By this, it provides an overview on the used techniques, on related monitoring efforts and on gained experiences.
