TY  - JOUR
TI  - 25 years of the Nigehörn constructed dune island (1989 - 2014): Outcomes of a disputed conservation project
AU  - Hellwig, U.
AU  - Körber, P.
AB  - 25 years after the construction of a new dune island in the Wadden Sea of Hamburg this measure can be seen as a success for nature conservation. The anthropogenically formed dune area of the Nigehörn island has retained a quite stable position and area despite sporadic fluctuations at the exposed borders. In contrast, the overall area has increased from less than 30 ha to more than 150 ha. Most of the new biotope areas consist of pioneer vegetation and salt marsh habitats, which are generally heterogeneous, diverse and rich in structure. However, dune succession is comparatively slow. The goal of creating lasting conditions to support breeding terns, in particular, was attained only for a few years in the early stages of the project. All in all, similarities to other aging dune islands in the German Bight are becoming apparent. For the near future, fusion with the neighbouring Scharhörn island appears highly probable, as does a maturation of the dunes, partly with various dune woodlands, and a diversification of the salt marshes.
