TY  - JOUR
TI  - Salt marsh sediments as natural resources for dike construction - Sediment recycling in clay pits
AU  - Karle Martina, M.
AU  - Bartholomä, A.
AB  - In the south-western Jade Bay, southern North Sea, the initial phase of the infilling process of a clay pit was investigated in a multidisciplinary study. The extraction of salt marsh sediments as material for dike construction is a growing source of conflict between coastal protection and conservation of natural habitats in the National Park Wadden Sea. At the study site, covering 10 ha of salt marsh, a volume of 150,000 m3 has been extracted for the purpose of dike construction. In order to better understand the temporal development of sedimentary and morphological patterns during the process of infilling of a salt marsh clay pit, the variation of grain size, dry bulk density and topographic features were analysed. Since 1999, when the clay pit was connected to the tidal drainage system of the Jade Bay in order to accelerate the process of infilling, sediment accumulated by settling out from suspension. Imported sediments are composed of 98% mud, i.e. grain sizes &gt;4 phi or &lt;0.063 mm. Clay contents (&gt;9 phi or &lt;0.002 mm) rarely exceed 50% of the mud fraction. The imported sediment thus shows the same grain-size distribution as that deposited on the open tidal flats north of the salt marsh belt. Soon after having connected the clay pit to the tidal drainage system of the Jade Bay, a meandering channel system developed. Therefore, with an increasing degree of filling, a self organized morphology developed within the clay pit, in contrast to the adjacent topography of the salt marsh dominated by linear drainage channels. Generally, vertical accretion rates were negatively correlated with elevation of the clay-pit surface. Today, the elevation of the surface substantially exceeds mean high water level (MHW). Therefore, flooding of the whole clay pit only takes place during high water spring tide and storm surges, and hence a decrease in sedimentation rate occurred from up to 15 cm/a during the first two years to less than 4 cm/a in 2007. A comparison of bulk density and water content of the clay pit sediments with a sub-recent clay pit and salt marsh sediments confirms that remarkable differences exist in physical parameters depending on age and use of the areas. With a mean dry bulk density of 0.5 g/ cm3, which is about half of that measured in salt marsh sediments, there is still a substantial deficit in sediment mass in the day pit compared to the adjacent marsh. © E. Schweizerbart'sche Verlagsbuchhandlung (Nägele u. Obermiller), 2008.
