TI  - Effect of sheep-grazing on soil physical properties of a coastal salt marsh (1): Saturated hydraulic conductivity and soil structure
AU  - Zhang, H.Q.
AU  - Horn, R.
AB  - To evaluate the soil use and management practises in the tidal wetland along the Wadden Sea coastline in Schleswig-Holstein, Germany, the response of soil structure and the saturated hydraulic conductivity to the different stocking rate of sheep-grazing was investigated. The results show that for Ksat there are no significant differences between no-grazing and extensive grazing (up to the stocking rate of 1,0 sheep unit pro hectare). Higher stocking rate leads to a significant reduction of Ksat. The effect of grazing on Ksat is constrained in the first 500 m near the dike and reached a soil depth of about 20-30 cm. The intensive grazing (stocking rate = 3,4 sheep units per hectare) can increase the bulk density 0,05 Mg m-3 in average compared with that of no-grazing. This increase is constrained within the most upper soil layer and in the range near the dike. The macropores decreased with increasing stocking rate while the mesopores increased. The micropores showed no response to grazing. It can be concluded that the structure of the salt marsh soil shows response to the sheep-grazing if the stocking rate is higher than 1,0 sheep unit per hectare. Due to the behaviour of grazing animals the effect on soil structure is constrained in the range near the dike. Furthermore, the effect can be identified down to a soil depth of 20-30 cm. The response of soil structure is a combination of change in pore size distribution and reduction of total porosity, and the first mechanism appears to be more pronounced.
