TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effect of sheep-grazing on the soil physical properties of a coastal salt marsh (2): Soil strength
AU  - Zhang, H.Q.
AU  - Horn, R.
AB  - To evaluate the soil use and management practises in the tidal wetland along the Wadden Sea coastline in Schleswig-Holstein, Germany, the relationship between soil strength properties and stocking rate of sheep-grazing was investigated. Results show that independent of the sampling time the vane shear strength firstly increased with increasing stocking rate and reached a maximum at a stocking rate of 1,0 sheep unit per hectare, which is about 30 to 50% higher than no-grazing. The increase of soil shear strength due to grazing is more pronounced near the dike and reached a soil depth of about 30 cm. This relation between soil strength and stocking rate is also shown by compression and direct shear tests. The precompression stress and effective cohesion of the surface soil (down to about 30 cm) increased with increasing stocking rate and reached the maximum at 1.0 sheep unit/ha. It was found that the soil had a high rebound potential and about 40% of the settlement at a given load was reversible. The change in root density, root morphology and soil structure due to grazing appears to acount for the increase in soil strength. With regard to the response of soil structure and soil strength to grazing, a stocking rate of 1,0 sheep unit per hectare for the tidal salt marsh can be recommended.
