TY  - JOUR
TI  - The nitrogen pilot project in Lower Saxony - Experiences gathered in the practical use of a nitrogen quota
AU  - Barunke, A.
AU  - Scheringer, J.
AU  - Köhne, M.
AB  - The Ministry of Agriculture in Lower Saxony initiated a eight-year research project to investigate how farmers react to a nitrogen-quota on farm-level (60 % according to a starting-year) and the effects on farmers' income and on the environment. Through the setting of a simple, central nitrogen quota on a farm-level basis it was anticipated that farmers would use innovative measures to bring about efficiency measures in their farm-management. The most important result of this research is that farmers adapted to the quota which was set and minimised income losses. The risk of polluting the environment with excess nitrogen was significantly reduced - especially on those farms with animal production. There, the nitrogen surplus was reduced by 47 % to 60 kg per hectare. However this instrument was not effective in protecting specific targets, like certain biotopes, or in enhancing the diversity of species on grasslands. The income changes varied from DM +36 per hectare till DM -566 per hectare on the less favourable locations in the region Luchow-Dannenberg and from DM + 141 per hectare till DM - 185 per hectare on the favourable locations in the region Weser-Ems. The level of income lost depended significantly on site-specific conditions like soil fertility and market conditions. According to the farmers involved in the project, the co-operation between the farmers, the consultants, the ministry and scientists promoted a positive, long-term effect on future farm management.
