TI  - Conserving and reintroducing the Atlantic sturgeon in North and Baltic Sea tributaries
AU  - Gessner, J.
AU  - Fredrich, F.
AU  - Arndt, G.-M.
AU  - Von Nordheim, H.
AB  - Sturgeons are among the most endan gered fish species worldwide. Their long life-span, late maturity and complex life cycle render them sensitive to anthropogenic impact. Both species of sturgeons that inhabited the North and Baltic Sea tributaries already underwent severe decline at the end of the 19th century. Today, Acipenser sturio is considered extinct in its former range with the exception of a small, declining population in the Gironde (France). Through support by the German Federal Agency for Nature Conservation, the prerequisites have been established for a recovery programme for both species by developing ex-situ po pulations of the species in Germany, verifying habitat suitability for early life stages, developing modifications of critical fishing techniques as well as assessing habitat utilisation via experimental stocking in the rivers designated for subsequent releases. The aim to achieve self-sustaining sturgeon populations is further supported by the establishment of a German National Action Plan for protection and remediation.
