TY  - JOUR
TI  - Implementing ocean literacy through the bond of informal and formal education
AU  - Niedoszytko, G.
AU  - Wojcieszek, D.
AU  - Podlesińska, W.
AU  - Borowiak, K.
AB  - Developing an ocean literate society is a long process. To pursue this goal, continuous cooperation between informal and formal education facilities should be sustained and complemented with the commitment of research institutes, authorities, decision makers, and stakeholders. This method of collaboration is demonstrated in the following chapter that describes a marine education program developed by the Gdynia Aquarium, a department of the National Marine Fisheries Research Institute (Poland). "Meet the Baltic Sea" (MBS) is a project designed to form an ocean-mindful society in Sopot City. Funded by the Municipality of Sopot, MBS began in 2004 and supports knowledge on and commitment to ocean protection. MBS complements the formal learning program from its very beginning and targets different educational stages. Each pupil in Sopot participates in workshops 6 times during their learning process. Thus, a student has systematic access to marine knowledge. The workshops' methods and materials are adjusted to the age of the pupils, beginning with "education through play" lessons in the early childhood education, through hands-on laboratory classes in the primary education, to practical tasks on board a hydrographic survey vessel in upper secondary school. The program presents the Baltic as an integral part of the ocean. Its fundamental goal is to build awareness of citizens' influence on the environment. The project aims to grow social awareness of the diversity of marine life and an inextricable interconnection of the ocean and people that should benefit in the sustainable growth of human and marine life. © Springer International Publishing AG, part of Springer Nature 2018.
