TY  - JOUR
TI  - Human impact on sedimentary dynamics on the East Frisian Island of Wangerooge
AU  - Zepp, H.
AU  - Kasielke, T.
AB  - Wangerooge, the easternmost of the East Frisian Islands, has been subject to intensive modifications of the island's morphology and morphodynamics by a variety of coastal protection measures over the past 140 years. This paper summarises the changes in the complex morphodynamic system, focussing on the more recent period. We discuss the interactions between natural forces (e.g. tidal and wind-induced currents, storm surges) and anthropogenic impacts (e.g. construction of groynes, beach nourishment, human induced dune formation). Our monitoring of coastal erosion during the last decade reveals severe erosion of the northeastern barrier dunes with a maximum retreat of the dune front of 48 m within one winter. In order to prevent further erosion, beach nourishment was carried out in the winter of 2009/10. The sand deposited amounted to 570,000 cbm. However, between March 2010 and 2011, two thirds of the sand were removed by longshore-drift. In the area of the bathing beach, artificial beach fill is carried out every year to preserve a beach attractive to the tourists. On average 65,000 cbm per year were dumped on the beach during the past 25 years, varying from less than 20,000 cbm to over 130,000 cbm per year. At this reach the amount of annually eroded sand shows a distinct correlation to the magnitude and frequency of storm surges and feeds the eastward beach with sediment. We conclude that even large efforts cannot prevent from the ongoing natural forming forces. Therefore, coastal protection produces 'eternal costs' - a fact that will exacerbate in the future due to the climate change and rising sea levels.
